text:
const user = { name: "Ada", age: 36 };
console.log(user.nmae);   // undefined - no error, no warning

const user: { name: string; age: number } = { name: "Ada", age: 36 };
console.log(user.nmae);   // compile error: Property 'nmae' does not exist



JavaScript sits inside TypeScript: TypeScript adds types, interfaces, generics, union types and strict mode, and after tsx has run only plain JavaScript is left

5.4 Basic types
let done: boolean = false;
let count: number = 42;          // one numeric type only - no int/long/double
let name: string = "Ada";        // ' and " are equivalent, ` allows interpolation
let nothing: undefined = undefined;
let empty: null = null;
Type inference means the annotation is usually unnecessary:
let count = 42;              // inferred as number
count = "seven";             // compile error
Annotate function parameters and return types, and leave local variables to inference.
function toCelsius(fahrenheit: number): number {
  return (fahrenheit - 32) / 1.8;
}

5.4.1 Template strings
const city = "Linz";
console.log(`It is ${toCelsius(72).toFixed(1)} °C in ${city}`);

5.5 any and unknown
any switches the type checker off for that value. It is contagious and almost always the wrong answer:
const data: any = JSON.parse(text);
data.whatever.at.all;     // compiles - crashes at runtime
unknown is the safe variant: you may hold the value, but you must narrow it before use.
const data: unknown = JSON.parse(text);
if (typeof data === "object" && data !== null && "name" in data) {
  // only here TypeScript lets you access data.name
}

5.6 Union types and literal types
When a value may only be one of a few fixed alternatives, TypeScript usually uses a union of string literal types rather than an enum:
type Stage = "main" | "tent" | "club";

let stage: Stage = "main";
stage = "garden";            // compile error
Advantages over enum: the value in the JSON file is the value in the code, no mapping needed, and it disappears completely at compile time.
Narrowing a union is called a type guard:
const STAGES = ["main", "tent", "club"] as const;

function isStage(value: string): value is Stage {
  return (STAGES as readonly string[]).includes(value);
}
That function is exactly the validation the compiler cannot do for you. You will write one in every exercise.

const names: string[] = ["Ada", "Grace", "Linus"];
const counts: Array&lt;number&gt; = [1, 2, 3];       // identical, rarely used

names.push("Alan");
console.log(names.length);   // 4

const list = [1, 2];
list.push(3);        // allowed
list = [4, 5];       // compile error

6.1.1 Useful basics
const first = names[0];
const last = names.at(-1);              // undefined if empty
const exists = names.includes("Ada");
const index = names.indexOf("Grace");   // -1 if not found
const part = names.slice(1, 3);         // copy, original untouched

:warning: names[99] is undefined at runtime, but TypeScript types it as string. This is a known hole in the type system. Be careful with indexed access.



6.1.2 Spread and destructuring
const more = [...names, "Barbara"];              // copy plus one element
const merged = [...names, ...counts.map(String)];

const [head, ...tail] = names;                   // head: string, tail: string[]
const { name, age } = user;                      // object destructuring
The spread operator is the normal way to copy an array or object instead of mutating it.


6.1.3 Tuples
A fixed-length array with a type per position, useful when a function has to return two values at once:
type Entry = [key: string, value: number];
const entry: Entry = ["temperature", 21.5];
const [key, value] = entry;

6.2 Functions
function add(a: number, b: number): number {
  return a + b;
}
Optional and default parameters:
function greet(name: string, greeting: string = "Hello", loud?: boolean): string {
  const text = `${greeting}, ${name}!`;
  return loud ? text.toUpperCase() : text;
}

There is no method overloading: a function name exists exactly once, and a second declaration replaces the first. Use optional parameters, or a union type for the parameter, when you need to accept more than one shape of input.


6.2.1 Functions are values
A function is a value like any other: it can be stored in a variable, passed as an argument and returned from another function. The type of a function is written as its parameter list plus =&gt; plus its return type.
type Formatter = (value: number) =&gt; string;

const asEuro: Formatter = (value) =&gt; `${value.toFixed(2)} €`;


6.2.2 Arrow functions
const double = (x: number): number =&gt; x * 2;      // expression body, implicit return
const log = (msg: string): void =&gt; {              // block body, explicit return
  console.log(msg);
};
Use arrow functions for everything you pass to an array method — that is what they are for.

import { readFileSync } from "fs";
import { readFile } from "fs/promises";

const bad = readFileSync("big.json", "utf-8");   // blocks the server. Never do this.
const good = await readFile("big.json", "utf-8"); // frees the thread while the disk works

7.2 The Promise
A Promise&lt;T&gt; is a value that is not there yet. It is pending, then either fulfilled with a value or rejected with an error.
async function loadCount(): Promise&lt;number&gt; {
  const text = await readFile("data.json", "utf-8");
  return JSON.parse(text).length;      // a returned value is wrapped in a Promise
}

const count = await loadCount();
Two rules:

A function that uses await must be declared async.
An async function always returns a Promise, whatever you write in the return type.


7.2.1 Forgetting await
const count = loadCount();          // Promise&lt;number&gt;, not a number
console.log(count);                 // Promise { &lt;pending&gt; }
console.log(count + 1);             // compile error - and it would be nonsense anyway
Nothing warns you at runtime — the program happily prints a Promise and carries on. The type checker is your only protection here, which is another reason never to switch it off.


7.2.2 Top-level await
Because we use ES modules, await works directly at the top level of a file. No main() wrapper is needed:
// src/app.ts
const data = await loadData();
console.log(data.length);


7.2.3 Sequential vs. parallel
const a = await loadA();        // waits
const b = await loadB();        // ... and only then starts

const [a, b] = await Promise.all([loadA(), loadB()]);   // both at once
Use Promise.all whenever the calls do not depend on each other. Two requests that each take 200 ms then cost you 200 ms, not 400.


7.2.4 Error handling
A rejected promise behaves like a thrown exception:
try {
  const text = await readFile(path, "utf-8");
} catch (error) {
  // error is of type `unknown` - you cannot just access error.message
  const message = error instanceof Error ? error.message : String(error);
  console.error(`Could not read ${path}: ${message}`);
}

:warning: In a catch block the error is unknown, not Error. Narrow it before use.

7.3 Reading files
import { readFile } from "fs/promises";

const text = await readFile("data/lineup.json", "utf-8");
Always pass the encoding. Without it you get a Buffer instead of a string.

7.3.1 Paths
A relative path is resolved against the working directory — wherever npm start was called from, which is not necessarily where your source file lives. Build paths explicitly:
import { dirname, join } from "path";
import { fileURLToPath } from "url";

const dirName = dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url));
const dataPath = join(dirName, "..", "data", "lineup.json");

:bulb: __dirname does not exist in ES modules. The three lines above are its replacement, and you will write them in every exercise from here on.



7.3.2 JSON
const text = await readFile(dataPath, "utf-8");
const acts = JSON.parse(text) as Act[];      // a LIE the compiler believes
JSON.parse returns any. The as Act[] tells the compiler to stop asking questions — it verifies nothing. If the file is malformed, or a field is missing, the program crashes somewhere far away from the actual cause.
For file data we write ourselves, as is acceptable. For anything coming from a user or a network, validate it with a type guard, as in EX2.


7.3.3 Writing files
import { writeFile } from "fs/promises";

const json = JSON.stringify(acts, null, 2);   // 2 = indentation, makes diffs readable
await writeFile(dataPath, json, "utf-8");
writeFile overwrites the whole file and creates it if it does not exist.


7.3.4 CSV
There is no built-in CSV parser. Split the text yourself:
import { EOL } from "os";

const lines = text.split(EOL).filter((line) =&gt; line.trim().length &gt; 0);
const rows = lines.slice(1);                 // skip the header
const tools = rows.map((line) =&gt; parseTool(line));

:warning: Use os.EOL, never a hard-coded "\n" — the files in this repository are edited on Windows and on Linux. If a file may come from either, split with /\r?\n/ instead.

7.4 Lazy loading and caching
Reading a file on every request is wasteful. Read it once, on first use, and keep it:
let acts: Act[] | null = null;              // null = not loaded yet

export async function getActs(): Promise&lt;Act[]&gt; {
  if (acts === null) {
    acts = await loadActsFromFile();
  }
  return acts;
}
Note the type Act[] | null — strict mode forces you to make “not loaded yet” explicit, and to handle it. This pattern shows up in EX3 and EX4.

7.5 Timers
setTimeout(() =&gt; console.log("later"), 1000);        // once, after 1 s
const handle = setInterval(() =&gt; tick(), 1000);      // every second
clearInterval(handle);                               // stop it - do not forget
setTimeout does not pause the program. The following line runs immediately; the callback runs later, when the thread is free.
console.log("a");
setTimeout(() =&gt; console.log("b"), 0);
console.log("c");
// prints a, c, b
Who does what, and when:



sequence diagram: app.ts logs A, hands the timer a callback, logs C, and only when the timer has expired does the callback log B


Your code hands the callback to the timer and carries straight on. The timer is not your thread, and the callback only gets its turn once your code has finished what it was doing.

8.3 A first Express server
npm install express http-status-codes
npm install --save-dev @types/express nodemon
src/app.ts:
import express, { Request, Response } from "express";
import { StatusCodes } from "http-status-codes";

const app = express();

app.use(express.json());        // parses a JSON request body into req.body

app.get("/", (req: Request, res: Response) =&gt; {
  res.send("hello world");
});

app.listen(3000, () =&gt; console.log("Server listening on http://localhost:3000"));
npm run dev        # nodemon restarts on every save

:bulb: express.json() is easy to forget. Without it req.body is undefined on every POST, and you will look for the bug in the wrong place.

8.4 Middleware
Express processes a request through a chain of functions. Each one may answer the request itself, or do a bit of work and hand it on to the next one with next().



the request passes through middleware 1, 2 and 3, each running some logic and calling next(), and the response travels back out through the same chain in reverse


Note the way back: after the last one has run, the response travels out through the same chain in reverse order.
app.use((req, res, next) =&gt; {
  console.log(`${req.method} ${req.url}`);
  next();                       // forget this and the request hangs forever
});

app.use(express.static("public"));   // serve static files from ./public
app.use("/api/acts", actRouter);     // mount a router under a path prefix
Order matters. The first matching handler wins.

8.5 Routers
Do not put your endpoints in app.ts. One router per resource, in its own file:
// src/act/act-router.ts
import { Router, Request, Response } from "express";
import { StatusCodes } from "http-status-codes";
import { findAll, findById } from "./act-service";

export const actRouter = Router();

actRouter.get("/", async (req: Request, res: Response) =&gt; {
  const stage = req.query.stage;             // string | string[] | undefined !
  res.json(await findAll(typeof stage === "string" ? stage : undefined));
});

actRouter.get("/:id", async (req: Request, res: Response) =&gt; {
  const act = await findById(req.params.id);

  if (act === undefined) {
    res.status(StatusCodes.NOT_FOUND).json({ error: `No act with id ${req.params.id}` });
    return;                                  // do NOT forget the return
  }

  res.json(act);
});
// src/app.ts
app.use("/api/acts", actRouter);
Three things to note:

req.params.id is always a string. Convert and validate it yourself.
req.query.stage is string | string[] | ParsedQs | undefined — a client can send ?stage=a&stage=b. Narrow it before use.
After res.status(...).json(...) you must return. Express does not stop the handler for you, and sending two responses is a runtime error.

8.7 DTOs
The objects you send over the wire are not your domain objects. A Tool class has methods and maybe internal state; what goes into the JSON is a plain, flat object — a Data Transfer Object.
export type ToolDto = {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  category: Category;
  available: boolean;
};

export function toToolDto(tool: Tool): ToolDto {
  return { id: tool.id, name: tool.name, category: tool.category, available: tool.isAvailable };
}
Why bother: the DTO is your API’s contract. It stays stable while the model behind it changes, and it lets you leave internal fields out.

const act = {
  id: "a01",
  name: "Kernel Panic",
  durationMinutes: 60,
};

console.log(act.name);
console.log(act["name"]); // the same thing

type Act = {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  durationMinutes: number;
  encore?: boolean; // optional -&gt; boolean | undefined
  readonly createdAt: string; // may only be set once, when the object is created
};

9.2 Structural typing (duck typing)
The single most important rule of the type system. TypeScript compares types by their shape, not by their name. Two types with the same members are the same type, even if they were declared separately and never mention each other.
type Point = { x: number; y: number };
type Coordinate = { x: number; y: number };

const p: Point = { x: 1, y: 2 };
const c: Coordinate = p; // fine. Same shape = same type.
Nothing has to implement anything. If it walks like a duck, it is a duck:
type Named = { name: string };

function greet(thing: Named) {
  console.log(`Hello ${thing.name}`);
}

const user = { name: "Ada", age: 36 };
greet(user); // fine - it HAS a name
greet(new Tool("t1", "Hammer")); // fine, if Tool has a `name` property

9.2.1 Excess property check
There is one exception that confuses everyone once:
const named: Named = { name: "Ada", age: 36 }; // ERROR: 'age' does not exist in 'Named'

const thing = { name: "Ada", age: 36 };
const named2: Named = thing; // fine!
A literal assigned directly is checked strictly, because extra properties there are almost always a typo. A value that already has a type is only checked for compatibility.

9.4 Classes
The syntax will look familiar. Three details are worth memorising, because they differ from what you would expect.
export class Tool {
  // 1. no access modifier means PUBLIC - there is no "internal"
  readonly id: string;
  readonly name: string;
  private timesLent = 0; // type inferred from the initialiser

  static readonly MAX_LOAN_DAYS = 14; // 2. no `const` in a class - use `static readonly`

  constructor(id: string, name: string) {
    this.id = id;
    this.name = name;
  }

  // 3. `get` is a method with a keyword, and it is called WITHOUT parentheses
  get isPopular(): boolean {
    return this.timesLent &gt; 10;
  }

  lend(): void {
    this.timesLent++;
  }
}

const tool = new Tool("t1", "Cordless drill");
console.log(tool.isPopular); // no ()

9.4.1 Parameter properties
The constructor boilerplate above has a short form:
export class Tool {
  constructor(
    readonly id: string,
    readonly name: string,
    private timesLent = 0
  ) {}
}
The modifier in the parameter list declares the field and assigns it. There is no other short form for this, and you will see it in a lot of TypeScript code.


9.4.2 private is not a security feature
class A {
  private secret = 42; // compile-time only - visible at runtime
  #reallySecret = 43; // truly private, enforced by the JavaScript runtime
}
private is erased with everything else during transpilation. (a as any).secret reads it without complaint. For our purposes private is fine — just do not mistake it for protection.


9.4.3 Static factory methods
A constructor cannot fail gracefully, cannot be async and cannot have overloads. The usual answer is a static factory:
export class Tool {
  private constructor(readonly id: string, readonly name: string) {}

  /** Returns undefined instead of throwing when the line is malformed. */
  static fromCsvLine(line: string): Tool | undefined {
    const [id, name] = line.split(";");
    return id && name ? new Tool(id, name) : undefined;
  }
}
Note the private constructor: it forces everyone through the factory, so the validation cannot be skipped. You will use this pattern in EX4.


9.4.4 Inheritance
class FullDayLoan extends Loan {
  constructor(id: string, toolId: string) {
    super(id, toolId); // must be called first
  }

  override describe(): string {
    // `override` is optional but recommended
    return `${super.describe()} (full day)`;
  }
}
abstract class, abstract members and implements all exist and behave as you expect. instanceof works at runtime, because classes — unlike interfaces and types — do survive transpilation.
We use inheritance sparingly in this unit. Prefer a union of types with a discriminating field:
type Loan =
  | { kind: "open"; toolId: string; since: string }
  | { kind: "returned"; toolId: string; since: string; until: string };

function describe(loan: Loan): string {
  // TypeScript narrows on `kind` - inside each branch the type is exact
  return loan.kind === "returned"
    ? `returned on ${loan.until}`
    : `open since ${loan.since}`;
}
This is a discriminated union, and it replaces a lot of small class hierarchies.

9.6 Map and Set
const byId = new Map&lt;string, Tool&gt;();
byId.set(tool.id, tool);
const found = byId.get("t1"); // Tool | undefined
byId.has("t1");
byId.delete("t1");
byId.size; // a property, not a method

for (const [id, tool] of byId) {
  // ...
}
const all = [...byId.values()];
A Map holds key/value pairs, a Set holds each value at most once. Build a Map from an array in one step:
const byId = new Map(tools.map((tool) =&gt; [tool.id, tool]));

:warning: A plain object ({}) also maps keys to values, but its keys are always strings, it inherits properties from Object.prototype, and it has no size. Use Map whenever the keys are data.

10.3 SQL we need

10.3.1 Creating a schema
CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS tools (
  id        TEXT PRIMARY KEY,
  name      TEXT NOT NULL,
  category  TEXT NOT NULL CHECK (category IN ('power', 'hand', 'measuring', 'garden')),
  condition TEXT NOT NULL CHECK (condition IN ('good', 'worn', 'broken'))
);

CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS loans (
  id       TEXT PRIMARY KEY,
  tool_id  TEXT NOT NULL REFERENCES tools (id),
  borrower TEXT NOT NULL,
  from_day TEXT NOT NULL,
  until    TEXT NOT NULL,
  state    TEXT NOT NULL CHECK (state IN ('open', 'returned'))
);
Three things the database now guarantees for you, which no amount of TypeScript could:

PRIMARY KEY — no two tools with the same id.
CHECK (… IN …) — the same constraint your union types express, but enforced at runtime.
REFERENCES tools (id) — a loan cannot point at a tool that does not exist.


:warning: SQLite only enforces foreign keys if you ask it to, per connection: db.exec("PRAGMA foreign_keys = ON"). Forgetting this is the classic SQLite mistake.

SQLite has very few types: TEXT, INTEGER, REAL, BLOB, NULL. There is no date type and no boolean. Store dates as TEXT in the format YYYY-MM-DD — it sorts correctly as a string — and booleans as INTEGER 0/1.
from and to are SQL keywords, which is why the column above is called from_day.


10.3.2 Queries
SELECT * FROM tools ORDER BY id;
SELECT * FROM tools WHERE category = ?;
SELECT * FROM tools WHERE id = ?;

INSERT INTO loans (id, tool_id, borrower, from_day, until, state) VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?);
UPDATE loans SET state = 'returned' WHERE id = ?;
DELETE FROM loans WHERE id = ?;
Filtering in WHERE instead of with filter in TypeScript is the point of using a database: the work happens where the data is, and an index can help.
-- the tools that are not lent out right now
SELECT t.* FROM tools t
WHERE t.condition &lt;&gt; 'broken'
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM loans l WHERE l.tool_id = t.id AND l.state = 'open');

10.4 Using the driver

10.4.1 Statements without a result
db.exec("PRAGMA foreign_keys = ON");
db.exec(schemaSql); // several statements at once


10.4.2 Prepared statements
const selectAll = db.prepare("SELECT * FROM tools ORDER BY id");
const rows = selectAll.all(); // an array of rows

const selectOne = db.prepare("SELECT * FROM tools WHERE id = ?");
const row = selectOne.get("t01"); // one row, or undefined

const insert = db.prepare("INSERT INTO loans (id, tool_id) VALUES (?, ?)");
const result = insert.run("l05", "t02"); // { changes, lastInsertRowid }
console.log(result.changes); // 1
changes tells you how many rows were affected — that is how you distinguish “updated” from “there was no such row”, and therefore how you decide between 200 and 404:
const result = db.prepare("UPDATE loans SET state = 'returned' WHERE id = ?").run(id);
if (result.changes === 0) {
  // no loan with that id
}


10.4.3 Never build SQL with string concatenation
// NEVER do this. This is an SQL injection.
db.exec(`SELECT * FROM tools WHERE id = '${userInput}'`);

// Always do this.
db.prepare("SELECT * FROM tools WHERE id = ?").get(userInput);
With ? placeholders the value is passed separately from the statement and can never be interpreted as SQL. A user who sends t01'; DROP TABLE tools; -- as an id then simply gets “not found”.


10.4.4 Rows are not your objects
const row = db.prepare("SELECT * FROM tools WHERE id = ?").get("t01");
row has the type unknown-ish and, at runtime, is a plain object whose keys are the column names — tool_id, not toolId. It has no methods. This is the same situation as JSON.parse, and it has the same answer: a row type plus a mapping function.
type ToolRow = {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  category: string;
  condition: string;
};

function toTool(row: ToolRow): Tool | undefined {
  return Tool.fromParts(row.id, row.name, row.category, row.condition);
}
Note that category is typed as string here, not as Category. The database CHECK constraint makes it very likely to be valid, but the driver cannot prove it — so we validate on the way in, exactly as we did with the CSV file.

10.5 Transactions
Several statements that must all succeed or all fail:
db.exec("BEGIN");
try {
  insertLoan.run(/* ... */);
  updateTool.run(/* ... */);
  db.exec("COMMIT");
} catch (error) {
  db.exec("ROLLBACK");
  throw error;
}
We need this only for the seeding script in the exercise, but it is the reason databases exist: either the whole change happens, or none of it.

10.6 Where the connection lives
Open the database once for the whole application, not per request:
// src/db.ts
import { DatabaseSync } from "node:sqlite";
import { dirname, join } from "path";
import { fileURLToPath } from "url";

const dirName = dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url));

export const db = new DatabaseSync(join(dirName, "..", "data", "workshop.db"));
db.exec("PRAGMA foreign_keys = ON");
The module body runs once, the first time the module is imported. That is the simplest singleton there is.

:bulb: Do not commit the .db file. Add it to .gitignore and commit the schema plus a seeding script instead — then anyone can recreate the database from scratch, which is exactly what you want in a repository.
